            <ArticleTitle>Asymptomatic Glomus Tumor of the Mediastinum.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>631625</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2015/631625</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Glomus tumors are rare benign neoplasms that predominate in limbs. Infrequently, they can occur in a wide anatomic distribution, to include sites not known to contain glomus cells. Although glomus tumors are usually small, pain and tenderness are common clinical symptoms. We report the case of a 69-year-old man with an asymptomatic large mediastinal glomus tumor, who underwent surgical resection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
